# dev-snapshot slim ruleset -- applied ON TOP of default.rules by --slim.
#
# Same format: kind <TAB> name <TAB> guard <TAB> why
#
# A slim snapshot is the material you wrote, and nothing that merely
# accumulated around it: no history, no logs, no archives, no binaries, no
# media. It is deliberately LOSSY, and it is not a substitute for a regular
# snapshot -- it is the one small enough to take often, or to carry.
#
# --slim also applies a per-file size cap (10M by default, --max-file-size to
# change it), because the categories below cannot name every large thing. Both
# the rules and the cap record what they dropped in the archive's manifest.

# --- history --------------------------------------------------------------
# The single biggest item, and the most deliberate omission: 2.7 GB on the
# tree this was written for. A regular snapshot exists to carry it. Never let
# a slim snapshot be the only copy of a repository with no remote.
dir	.git	-	repository history
dir	.hg	-	repository history
dir	.svn	-	repository history

# --- logs and dumps -------------------------------------------------------
dir	logs	-	log directory
dir	log	-	log directory
file	*.log	-	log file
file	*.log.[0-9]	-	rotated log
file	*.out	-	captured output
file	core.[0-9]*	-	core dump
file	*.sql	-	database dump
file	*.dump	-	database dump
file	*.sqlite	-	embedded database
file	*.sqlite3	-	embedded database
file	*.db	-	embedded database

# --- archives -------------------------------------------------------------
file	*.tar	-	archive
file	*.tar.*	-	archive
file	*.tgz	-	archive
file	*.zip	-	archive
file	*.gz	-	archive
file	*.bz2	-	archive
file	*.xz	-	archive
file	*.zst	-	archive
file	*.7z	-	archive
file	*.rar	-	archive
file	*.jar	-	java archive
file	*.war	-	java archive
file	*.whl	-	python wheel
file	*.phar	-	php archive
file	*.gpg	-	encrypted blob

# --- built and downloaded binaries ----------------------------------------
file	*.so	-	shared object
file	*.so.[0-9]*	-	shared object
file	*.dylib	-	shared object
file	*.dll	-	shared object
file	*.a	-	static library
file	*.o	-	object file
file	*.class	-	compiled java
file	*.exe	-	executable
file	*.AppImage	-	bundled application
file	*.deb	-	package
file	*.rpm	-	package
file	*.iso	-	disk image
file	*.img	-	disk image
file	*.dmg	-	disk image

# --- media ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Images are kept: a diagram, a screenshot in a README and a logo are material.
# Video and audio are not, and are never small.
file	*.mp4	-	video
file	*.mov	-	video
file	*.avi	-	video
file	*.mkv	-	video
file	*.webm	-	video
file	*.mp3	-	audio
file	*.wav	-	audio
file	*.flac	-	audio
file	*.psd	-	large source image
file	*.mmdb	-	geoip database

# --- deliberately NOT here ------------------------------------------------
#
# *.csv     A CSV is usually data somebody produced, and the size cap catches
#           the exports without dropping a small lookup table that is source.
# *.pdf     A document. That is what a slim snapshot is for.
# *.png     A diagram or a screenshot in a README is material.
# .env      Still the least replaceable file in most trees.
